.TH DATE 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
date \- print or set the system date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR seconds " |"
.B \-d
.BI @ seconds\fR]\fP
.RB [ + \fIformat\fP ]
.br
.B date
.RB [ \-u ]
.RI [[[[[ cc ] yy ] mm ] dd ] hh ] mm [. ss ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B date
writes the current date and time, or another given time, to standard
output.
.PP
The output is produced by
.BR strftime (3)
with the format
.I format
if an argument beginning with
.B +
is given, or
.B \(dq%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y\(dq
otherwise, followed by a newline.
The result may be at most 255 bytes long.
.PP
Local time is computed from the
.B TZ
environment variable; without it, local time is UTC.
.SS Setting the clock
An operand made of digits, with an optional
.BI . ss
suffix, sets the system clock with
.BR stime (2),
which requires superuser privileges.
The digits before the dot are read as two-digit fields, from the most
significant field present down to the minute:
.TP
.I mm
minute;
.TP
.I hhmm
hour and minute;
.TP
.I ddhhmm
day of month, hour, minute;
.TP
.I mmddhhmm
month, day, hour, minute;
.TP
.I yymmddhhmm
two-digit year, month, day, hour, minute;
.TP
.I ccyymmddhhmm
four-digit year, month, day, hour, minute.
.PP
Fields that are not given are taken from the current date; the
seconds default to 0.
A two-digit year from 69 to 99 means 1969 to 1999, and 00 to 68 means
2000 to 2068.
Out-of-range fields (a month outside 1 to 12, a day outside 1 to 31,
an hour above 23, a minute above 59, or seconds above 61) are
rejected.
The time is interpreted as local time, or as UTC with
.BR \-u .
After the clock is set, the new time is printed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-u
Display, and interpret a setting operand, in UTC instead of local
time.
.TP
.BI \-r " seconds"
Display the time
.I seconds
seconds after the Epoch (1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC) instead of the
current time.
The argument may also be attached
.RB ( \-r1700000000 ).
.TP
.BI "\-d @" seconds
Same as
.BR \-r ;
this is the only form of
.B \-d
argument accepted.
.TP
.BI + format
Use
.I format
as the
.BR strftime (3)
output format.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid usage, an invalid argument, a failure to set the clock, or an
over-long formatted result.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
POSIX timezone string used for local time.
If unset or empty, local time is UTC.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the date in ISO 8601 form:
.PP
.EX
$ date +%Y\-%m\-%d
.EE
.PP
Convert an Epoch time to a readable UTC date:
.PP
.EX
$ date \-u \-r 1700000000
Tue Nov 14 22:13:20 UTC 2023
.EE
.PP
Set the clock to 14:30 today:
.PP
.EX
# date 1430
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stime (2),
.BR time (2),
.BR strftime (3)
.SH BUGS
The setting operand orders its fields year first, as in BSD
.BR date ,
not as the POSIX
.I mmddhhmm[[cc]yy]
form.
.PP
The day of month is checked only against 1 to 31, so a date such as
February 31 is silently normalised into the following month.
.PP
.B \-d
does not parse free-form date strings.
